# ==========================================================================
# WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR
# ==========================================================================
# This project is deployed by CLONING it onto each laptop.  A clone must be
# runnable immediately -- no file to copy by hand, no interactive login, no
# "it works on my machine".  So the rule here is deliberately narrow:
#
#     IGNORE only what a run CREATES and can create again by itself,
#     plus the collected data, which belongs to one laptop and not to git.
#
#     TRACK everything else -- code, configuration, documentation, the test
#     fixtures, and the GitHub login.
#
# If you are about to add a line to this file, ask: "would a fresh clone
# still start without it?"  If the answer is no, do not add the line.
#
# VERIFY IT after any change.  Simulate a clone and run the three read-only
# modes; none of them writes anything:
#
#     git ls-files -z | xargs -0 tar c | (mkdir -p /tmp/t && tar x -C /tmp/t)
#     cd /tmp/t
#     python3 student_scraper.py --validate-keywords     # expect RESULT: PASS
#     python3 student_scraper.py --dry-run               # expect search 1 of N
#     ./reset.sh -n                                      # expect no errors


# ==========================================================================
# 1. TRACKED ON PURPOSE -- do not add ignore rules for these
# ==========================================================================
# Listed here as documentation.  Nothing below should ever match them.
#
#   student_scraper.py      the scraper
#   config.py               every path and tuning constant
#   new_mylib.py            the cities and the keywords.  Configuration, not
#                           output: its history is what explains why a run
#                           searched what it did.
#   requirements.txt        the dependency list
#   reset.sh                returns a clone to its freshly-cloned state
#   README.md, Documentation/
#   tests/                  including tests/fixtures/*.xlsx -- real Instant
#                           Data Scraper exports the suite depends on.
#                           Without them the suite silently loses coverage.
#   github_profile.txt      one real captured profile, read by
#                           tests/test_merge.py
#
#   github_auth.json        THE GITHUB LOGIN.  Tracked DELIBERATELY.
#
#       This file used to be ignored, and that was the one thing stopping a
#       clone from working out of the box: without it the crawl starts,
#       fails to detect a login and stops with "Run: python3
#       student_scraper.py --login" -- which opens a browser window and
#       therefore needs a machine with a display.  On a headless box, or on
#       eight laptops at once, that is not workable.
#
#       UNDERSTAND WHAT IS NOW IN GIT.  This file IS the session: roughly
#       12 github.com cookies including user_session.  Anyone who can read
#       this repository is signed in as that account, with no password and
#       no 2FA prompt.  The repository is private, which is what makes this
#       acceptable; it is not a secret that can be shared further.
#
#       Two practical consequences, neither of them optional:
#         - Git history is permanent.  Deleting the file in a later commit
#           does NOT remove it from history.  Rotating the account's session
#           (sign out everywhere on GitHub) is what actually invalidates it.
#         - GitHub rotates user_session periodically, so a committed copy
#           goes stale.  When a clone reports "GitHub login not detected",
#           run --login once on a machine with a display and commit the
#           refreshed file.
#
#       If this repository is ever made public, remove this file from
#       tracking and rotate the session the same day.


# ==========================================================================
# 2. PER-MACHINE CRAWL STATE -- ignored, and it MUST stay that way
# ==========================================================================
# These are not ignored for secrecy.  They are ignored because tracking them
# would BREAK the thing this file is trying to achieve.
#
# One folder is one rank.  Each laptop has its own position in its own
# campaign, and every one of these files is recreated automatically when it
# is absent -- read_current_row() writes current_row.json at START,
# StateManager treats a missing file as a fresh run, load_cycle_num()
# returns 0, and the store is created with its schema on first open.
#
# So a clone with none of them present is EXACTLY what "start fresh" means.
# Commit one and every laptop that pulls inherits another laptop's crawl
# position, duplicate index and cooldown -- they would all resume at the
# same search and collect the same people.
scraper_state.json
current_row.json
cycle_state.json
keyword_cycle.json
deferred_profiles.json
transient_failures.json
throttle_state.json
rate_limit_state.json
temporary.txt
temporary.json
skipped_accounts.txt
failed_profiles.txt

# The result store: what makes a duplicate a duplicate.  WAL mode, so the
# two side files travel with it.
students.db
students.db-wal
students.db-shm
students.db-journal

# THIS LAPTOP'S RANK.  {"rank": 2, "num_sys": 3}
#
# Ignored for a specific reason, and it is the one that makes `git pull`
# usable here.  `rank` differs per laptop.  While it was edited directly in
# student_scraper.py, every laptop with rank != 1 had a locally modified
# TRACKED file, so the moment anything else in that file changed upstream
# the pull refused outright:
#
#     error: Your local changes to the following files would be
#     overwritten by merge:  student_scraper.py
#     Aborting
#
# Keeping the per-machine number in an ignored file removes the collision
# entirely: pull only ever touches tracked files, and this is not one.
# rank.example.json IS tracked, so a new laptop can see the format.
rank.json


# ==========================================================================
# 3. COLLECTED PERSONAL DATA -- ignored
# ==========================================================================
# Real people's names, emails, phone numbers and WhatsApp numbers.  This is
# the output of a run, it is specific to one laptop, and committing it would
# push one machine's collected people onto every other machine.
#
# The directory itself is recreated by ensure_dirs() on every startup, so a
# clone without it still runs.
Student Data original/
backup_final_output_*/
backup_legacy_migration_*/
*.xlsx.legacy

# Loose workbooks dropped at the top level by hand or by Instant Data
# Scraper.  Root-anchored (leading /) so tests/fixtures/*.xlsx is untouched.
/student_data.xlsx
/student_data_*.xlsx
/github*.xlsx


# ==========================================================================
# 4. WORKING DIRECTORIES -- ignored, all recreated on demand
# ==========================================================================
# ensure_runtime_dirs() creates the first three on every startup;
# staging_for() creates crawl_staging/<slug>/ when a search needs staging;
# --retry-current-search creates retry_backup/; --migrate-output creates
# imported/; _connect() creates history/ when something first writes to it.
temp_downloads/
keyword_downloads/
crawl_staging/
retry_backup/
logs/
history/
imported/

# The persistent Chromium profile.  Holds login cookies and ~1 GB of cache,
# and the current design keeps it OUTSIDE the project anyway (config.py
# builds it under /var/tmp).  This rule covers the legacy in-project path.
.pw_profile/


# ==========================================================================
# 5. JUNK -- ignored
# ==========================================================================
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.venv/
venv/

# Atomic writes land on "<path>.tmp" and on the hidden "<dir>/.<name>.tmp"
# sibling before being renamed over the target.  An interrupted write leaves
# one behind; neither spelling belongs in git.
*.tmp
.*.tmp

# LibreOffice lock files, created while a .docx or .xlsx is open.  This
# project never deletes them and never commits them.
.~lock.*#

# Editor and OS clutter.
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/


# ==========================================================================
# 6. EXPLICIT RESCUES
# ==========================================================================
# Belt and braces: these are needed to run or to test, and a future rule
# above must not be able to swallow them by accident.
!tests/fixtures/*.xlsx
!tests/fixtures/*.json
!github_auth.json
!requirements.txt
!rank.example.json
